  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remaining dry and seasonably mild today.\n\nMorning sfc analysis shows a large, diffuse area of high pressure \nover most of the southeastern CONUS. Well to our W, a shortwave \ntrough aloft is digging through the central Plains and the \nbeginnings of sfc low pressure development are noted over the \neastern Dakotas and Iowa vicinity. While this system will eventually \nbe a player in our weather tomorrow, one last day of pleasant and \nmild weather is on the way. High temps will generally be in the \nupper 60s to lower 70s W of the Chesapeake Bay and in the mid to \nupper 60s on the Eastern Shore. Some increase in high clouds is \nlikely this afternoon as upper-level moisture begins to impinge into \nthe area. Tonight's lows generally in the 40s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A series of cold fronts brings a chance for isolated \nto scattered showers Thursday, but any potential for appreciable \nrain is low. Much cooler temperatures follow behind the fronts with \nbelow freezing overnight temperatures likely inland this weekend.\n\nStill expecting a pronounced pattern shift over the next few days, \ncharacterized by a chance for scattered showers Thursday and then \nmuch cooler temps and gusty winds Friday into the weekend. Regarding \nthe rainfall potential, the most likely for any showers has shifted \nto the Thursday morning and afternoon timeframe as the lead \nshortwave pivots into the region. A lingering shower or two is also \npossible into the evening Thursday. Forecast rainfall amounts are \nvery uninspiring and currently appear to be a tenth of an inch or \nless. The best shot for any showers is confined to the northern half \nof our forecast area, with chances tapering off quickly down near \nthe NC/VA border. Any rain also appears unlikely Friday now as drier \nair infiltrates the area and the associated sfc low moves offshore \nof New England. \n\nThe main focus will be the much cooler temperatures as an \nanomalously strong longwave trough moves into the region. Thursday \nlooks to be the last mild day for a while highs in the mid-upper 60s \nfor most and around 70F SE. Cooler air rushes in Thursday \nevening/night behind a series of cold fronts and forecast highs \nFriday are only in the 50s. The large upper trough remains parked \nover most of the eastern CONUS Saturday, and this still looks like \nthe coolest day of the period with highs only expected to reach the \nlower 50s for most of the area. It may struggle to get out of the \nupper 40s across the far northern portions of the area and \nespecially the lower MD Eastern Shore. These cool temps continue \ninto early next week. Overnight lows will also be much cooler with \nlows in the upper 20s to around freezing for most locations away \nfrom the coast Friday, Saturday, and Sunday nights. Subfreezing \ntemperatures may continue (for lows) Monday and Tuesday nights as \nwell. Accordingly, the growing season looks to be ending shortly for \nmost areas. Will likely need a Freeze Watch as we get closer to the \nweekend. Gusty NW winds could make these temps feel even chillier.",
